WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.468 --> 00:00:02.668
-Welcome back, Marty.
It's so good to see you.

00:00:02.668 --> 00:00:05.038
-Good to see you, Seth.
You look fantastic.

00:00:05.038 --> 00:00:08.307
I see that you've gotten rid
of all your Confederate flags.

00:00:08.307 --> 00:00:09.608
Good for you.

00:00:09.608 --> 00:00:11.944
-[ Laughing, stammering ]
It was time.

00:00:11.944 --> 00:00:13.579
We decided it was time.

00:00:13.579 --> 00:00:15.581
-Well, I'm very excited
to be here, you know.

00:00:15.581 --> 00:00:18.218
As you know, there's no --
I think

00:00:18.218 --> 00:00:23.556
of all the hosts that pretend
to care about their guests,

00:00:23.556 --> 00:00:25.991
you're the one
who pretends the best.

00:00:25.991 --> 00:00:27.326
-Oh, well, thank you.

00:00:27.326 --> 00:00:30.029
You know, I think I really
put that skill to the test

00:00:30.029 --> 00:00:33.532
every time you're here. So --

00:00:33.532 --> 00:00:35.468
-Th-Thanks. Thank you.

00:00:35.468 --> 00:00:37.903
-[ Laughs ]
-No, listen, no, I love --

00:00:37.903 --> 00:00:40.373
You know, you and I,

00:00:40.373 --> 00:00:42.341
I think we click. Don't you?

00:00:42.341 --> 00:00:44.077
-Yes. Yes.

00:00:44.077 --> 00:00:45.845
-Whether it's "SNL"
or "Late Night"

00:00:45.845 --> 00:00:50.049
or the back of Jeffrey Epstein's
plane, you know.

00:00:50.049 --> 00:00:51.350
-Yeah.

00:00:51.350 --> 00:00:53.486
-Who was that lady friend
of yours?

00:00:53.486 --> 00:00:57.690
How do you pronounce her name,
"Jislane"? "Galane"?

00:00:57.690 --> 00:00:59.359
-It's Ghislaine,
but not a friend.

00:00:59.359 --> 00:01:00.827
We're just --
-Ghislaine! You two were --

00:01:00.827 --> 00:01:02.829
You two were on a roll.

00:01:02.829 --> 00:01:04.863
You were playing that --
I remember she was knitting,

00:01:04.863 --> 00:01:06.532
and you were holding the wool,
and you were laughing.

00:01:06.532 --> 00:01:08.234
And I couldn't hear what it was.
-[ Laughs ]

00:01:08.234 --> 00:01:11.738
-But I could tell you were
just -- Something was happening.

00:01:11.738 --> 00:01:14.107
-[ Laughs ]
-Anyway.

00:01:14.107 --> 00:01:15.741
-And, you know,
I know you're busy.

00:01:15.741 --> 00:01:17.910
Thank you for making
the time tonight.

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:19.879
-Oh, please. No, no, I realized

00:01:19.879 --> 00:01:22.849
that I had not been on your show
for over a year,

00:01:22.849 --> 00:01:25.585
which is fascinating that --
how time flies

00:01:25.585 --> 00:01:28.221
when you're ghost-writing
for Tucker Carlson.

00:01:28.221 --> 00:01:30.123
-Yeah.
-It zooms by.

00:01:30.123 --> 00:01:33.159
-[ Chuckles ]
-But I love your show, Seth.

00:01:33.159 --> 00:01:37.263
And I wish
I could do it more, really.

00:01:37.263 --> 00:01:40.199
But...pride.

00:01:40.199 --> 00:01:41.935
-Yeah. No, I --

00:01:41.935 --> 00:01:43.769
I could see
how that would get in the way.

00:01:43.769 --> 00:01:45.538
-Hey, there was something
you said to me earlier

00:01:45.538 --> 00:01:47.306
before we started.

00:01:47.306 --> 00:01:50.009
So, you think they should
keep the name Redskins.

00:01:50.009 --> 00:01:51.110
Why is that?

00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:53.379
-[ Laughing ]
No, I did not say that.

00:01:53.379 --> 00:01:54.613
I didn't.
-Well, then

00:01:54.613 --> 00:01:55.882
someone said it to me.

00:01:55.882 --> 00:01:57.850
-[ Laughs ]

00:01:57.850 --> 00:01:59.552
-Who was it?

00:01:59.552 --> 00:02:04.190
Oh! It's my --
the people who do my patio.

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:06.025
-How have you been
spending your time

00:02:06.025 --> 00:02:09.862
with the extra time
that all of us have?

00:02:09.862 --> 00:02:12.164
-[ Chuckling ] Well, you know,

00:02:12.164 --> 00:02:15.300
the UPS guy and I are engaged,
if that's what you --

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:17.203
-Oh, congratulations.

00:02:17.203 --> 00:02:19.772
-No, I'm on a very similar
schedule each day.

00:02:19.772 --> 00:02:23.609
I get up around 8:00.
I have breakfast. I exercise.

00:02:23.609 --> 00:02:28.014
And then it's around 12:15
at night, get ready for bed,

00:02:28.014 --> 00:02:29.882
and then it's time for Corden.

00:02:29.882 --> 00:02:33.152
-[ Laughs ]

00:02:33.152 --> 00:02:35.054
-I love traditions.

00:02:35.054 --> 00:02:37.122
-Yeah, it's true.
-You can have fun with the --

00:02:37.122 --> 00:02:39.725
You know, and I, again,
follow the scientists.

00:02:39.725 --> 00:02:42.595
In fact, I'm keeping
12 feet away from people

00:02:42.595 --> 00:02:45.665
so I don't get coronavirus
next year.

00:02:45.665 --> 00:02:47.033
See, I'm thinking ahead.
-Oh, that's smart.

00:02:47.033 --> 00:02:51.037
You did appear
in one of the Zoom "SNL,"

00:02:51.037 --> 00:02:54.607
the three of those
that happened this spring.

00:02:54.607 --> 00:02:57.510
You got to perform with Heidi
Gardner, who is just fantastic.

00:02:57.510 --> 00:02:59.979
-She is like a genius.
Don't you think she's fantastic?

00:02:59.979 --> 00:03:03.949
-She is. There's real "SCTV" DNA
to the way she performs.

00:03:03.949 --> 00:03:06.653
Don't you think that's accurate?
-I do agree with that, yeah.

00:03:06.653 --> 00:03:09.355
It's -- She's so committed.

00:03:09.355 --> 00:03:12.892
And when we did it, it was a
Zoom about, you know, two idiots

00:03:12.892 --> 00:03:15.694
who had been to Italy
during the pandemic.

00:03:15.694 --> 00:03:17.963
And she was making me laugh
every take,

00:03:17.963 --> 00:03:20.032
because she was
getting her face all...

00:03:20.032 --> 00:03:21.800
like this.
-[ Laughs ]

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:24.770
It was just fantastic.

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:27.473
And, you know, Beck and Kenan
and Aidy and Melissa,

00:03:27.473 --> 00:03:29.408
they were all just so good.

00:03:29.408 --> 00:03:36.048
-It's crazy to me that you were
only on "SNL" for one year,

00:03:36.048 --> 00:03:39.819
because you certainly
made a mark in your time there.

00:03:39.819 --> 00:03:41.153
Looking back at it,

00:03:41.153 --> 00:03:44.590
do you wish it had been
a longer tenure at the show?

00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:46.725
-I do. I wish I'd --

00:03:46.725 --> 00:03:48.261
I had a one-year contract.

00:03:48.261 --> 00:03:50.529
Billy Crystal
and Christopher Guest

00:03:50.529 --> 00:03:53.099
and Harry Shearer and I
and Rich Hall

00:03:53.099 --> 00:03:54.600
had one-year contracts.

00:03:54.600 --> 00:03:57.769
And I think at that time,
Dick Ebersol -- It wasn't Lorne.

00:03:57.769 --> 00:04:01.274
Lorne had taken his five-year
sabbatical from the show.

00:04:01.274 --> 00:04:03.042
They were just trying
to keep it alive.

00:04:03.042 --> 00:04:06.546
And Eddie Murphy had left.

00:04:06.546 --> 00:04:09.281
And so, anyway,
we got one-year contracts.

00:04:09.281 --> 00:04:13.852
And I think it was so stressful
for me every week,

00:04:13.852 --> 00:04:16.789
because I treated it like
final exams every week.

00:04:16.789 --> 00:04:19.825
I think if I'd known I was going
to be there for five years,

00:04:19.825 --> 00:04:22.461
I would have taken it easy.
But every --

00:04:22.461 --> 00:04:24.397
You know, and I was
a writer, too, so.

00:04:24.397 --> 00:04:26.665
And Billy Crystal always tells
one of his favorite moments is

00:04:26.665 --> 00:04:29.368
it's 2:00 in the morning,

00:04:29.368 --> 00:04:31.337
and the read-through
is the next day.

00:04:31.337 --> 00:04:33.505
And in those days, you had to
slide the script under the door,

00:04:33.505 --> 00:04:35.974
'cause they had to be typed.
-Yeah.

00:04:35.974 --> 00:04:38.510
-So you had to slide it
by 7:00 a.m., I think,

00:04:38.510 --> 00:04:40.213
on Wednesday morning.

00:04:40.213 --> 00:04:42.381
And so, it's Wednesday morning,
I'm sorry,

00:04:42.381 --> 00:04:43.916
about 4:00 in the morning,

00:04:43.916 --> 00:04:46.184
and he's leaving, and he knocks
on my office door,

00:04:46.184 --> 00:04:49.989
and he says, "How is it going?"
I said, "Oh, good."

00:04:49.989 --> 00:04:52.792
And he looks over my shoulder,
and it says,

00:04:52.792 --> 00:04:55.827
"Ed Grimley's apartment,
open on."

00:04:55.827 --> 00:04:57.196
Nothing else written.

00:04:57.196 --> 00:04:58.764
[ Both laugh ]

00:04:58.764 --> 00:05:02.168
Complete and utter panic
and despair.

00:05:02.168 --> 00:05:04.203
Then you have
a great show Saturday,

00:05:04.203 --> 00:05:06.105
and then 48 hours later,
you feel like a failure,

00:05:06.105 --> 00:05:09.708
'cause you have no new ideas
for Ringo, the host, or whoever.

00:05:09.708 --> 00:05:13.346
-Yeah, it was not a -- Success
was not a domino at "SNL."

00:05:13.346 --> 00:05:15.081
Like, it very much fell

00:05:15.081 --> 00:05:18.417
and did not connect in any way,
shape, or form to the next show.

00:05:18.417 --> 00:05:21.954
Like, you could be as hot
as possible on one Saturday

00:05:21.954 --> 00:05:24.523
and then just ice cold.
-But do you think I'm --

00:05:24.523 --> 00:05:26.659
Do you think -- Or is it just
what everyone feels?

00:05:26.659 --> 00:05:28.494
In other words, that sense of,

00:05:28.494 --> 00:05:30.196
"Had I known
I was going to stay longer,

00:05:30.196 --> 00:05:33.999
I would have chilled more," or
would I have been just as tense?

00:05:33.999 --> 00:05:36.636
-I think you'd be just as tense
because even though

00:05:36.636 --> 00:05:39.005
the contracts people sign now
are longer,

00:05:39.005 --> 00:05:41.440
it doesn't mean that you can't
get fired after a year.

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:42.942
And I think most people
after a few episodes

00:05:42.942 --> 00:05:44.777
start thinking that way.
-No one fires the Marty Short.

00:05:44.844 --> 00:05:46.712
No one fires the Marty Short.

00:05:46.712 --> 00:05:49.114
-[ Laughs ]

00:05:49.114 --> 00:05:50.382
-I know what you mean.

00:05:50.382 --> 00:05:51.884
I know what you mean.
-Yes.

00:05:51.884 --> 00:05:55.254
-But I'm talking about my world
and my life.

